  [   15.2-   17.4] Now, let me, in the last couple of lectures,
  [   17.6-   21.1] I've mentioned a key theme of the lectures
  [   21.1-   24.8] which is the two main responsibilities of central banks,
  [   25.3-   28.4] financial stability and economic stability.
  [   28.6-   30.4] Let me turn it around just a bit and talk
  [   30.4-   33.5] about the two main tools for financial stability,
  [   33.5-   36.8] the main tool that central banks have is lender of last resort powers.
  [   37.0-   40.6] By providing short-term liquidity to financial institutions
  [   40.6-   46.0] for placing lost funding, central banks, as they have for now a number
  [   46.0-   49.2] of centuries, can help calm a financial panic.
  [   49.9-   53.5] For economic stability, the principal tool is monetary policy.
  [   54.0-   55.1] Of course, in normal times,
  [   55.1-   58.1] that involves adjusting the level of short-term interest rates.
  [   59.1-   62.6] Now, today, I'm going to be focusing primarily on the intense phase
  [   62.6-   68.0] of the financial crisis in 2008-2009, and so I'll be focusing primarily
  [   68.0-   70.8] on the lender of last resort function of the central bank.
  [   71.4-   74.1] I'll come back to monetary policy in the final lecture when we talk
  [   74.1-   75.9] about the aftermath and the recovery.
  [   78.3-   83.4] Now, last time, this is just a repeat from last time, I talked about some
