  [  109.8-  116.3] including the excessive debt taken on, perhaps because of the period of the great moderation.
  [  116.9-  120.5] Very importantly, the banks' inability to monitor their own risks,
  [  121.9-  125.3] excessive reliance on short-term funding, which as a bank
  [  125.3-  129.4] in the 19th century would tell you makes it vulnerable to a run
  [  129.4-  133.0] as short-term funding is pulled away, and increased use
  [  133.0-  135.5] of exotic financial instruments like credit default swaps
  [  135.5-  138.7] and others that concentrated risk in particular companies
  [  138.7-  139.8] or in particular markets.
  [  140.4-  141.7] So that was the private sector.
  [  141.8-  143.5] The public sector had its own vulnerabilities,
  [  144.1-  146.1] including gaps in the regulatory structure
  [  146.1-  149.0] important firms and markets did not have adequate oversight.
  [  149.7-  153.0] Where there was adequate oversight, at least in law,
  [  153.4-  155.0] sometimes the supervisors
  [  155.0-  157.1] and regulators didn't do a good enough job.
  [  157.5-  159.9] For example, there wasn't enough attention paid
